{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gigo6hnhpsgzlr73bijf7apjlkjag3h3pjugbdg3kxen5q37mmea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1356,"cache_read_input_tokens":71426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1356,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0166fexdEz6yfUVzz3kjTvvL","session_id":"6a91fdee-45d7-4693-8d7e-88ac17e9e078","uuid":"5ccecd3b-772c-45f0-9b97-2d134100de05","timestamp":"2026-08-26T21:44:38.511Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Independent C-vs-Rust semantic audit"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gigo6hnhpsgzlr73bijf7apjlkjag3h3pjugbdg3kxen5q37mmea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict\n\n**NO BEHAVIORAL DIFFERENCES FOUND.**\n\nThe Rust `encode_quant` is behaviorally identical to the C `encode_quant` for all i32 input tuples, to the limits of what I could verify (see \"Unsure\" below).\n\nFiles audited: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-s8YUb3/c_src/src/lib.c`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-s8YUb3/c_src/include/lib.h`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-s8YUb3/translation/src/lib.rs`.\n\n### Item-by-item results\n\n**1. `diff` expression / method-chain vs C precedence — MATCH.** The chain parses as `((((2).wrapping_mul(uni & 7)).wrapping_add(1)).wrapping_mul(step)) / 8`, i.e. `(2*(uni&7) + 1) * step` then `/8` — the same associativity and the same single wrap point (the `* step`; `2*(uni&7)+1` is bounded by 15 and cannot wrap) as C line 30. Verified **exhaustively over all 2^32 `step` values × all 8 possible `uni & 7` values (34.4 billion pairs)** via FNV-1a checksums compared between rustc-compiled and gcc-compiled code. I injected a single off-by-one canary to prove the checksum actually detects a one-element difference (it did); an earlier rotate+XOR checksum was GF(2)-linear and degenerate, so I discarded it.\n\n**2. `~7`/`!7` and `~1`/`!1` — MATCH.** Rust `!7i32 == -8 == ~7`; `!1i32 == -2 == ~1`. Type inference makes both i32 (they are ANDed with i32 operands).\n\n**3. Signed shifts `>>31 >>1 >>2 >>5` — MATCH.** Arithmetic in both. Verified exhaustively over all 2^32 inputs, plus spot checks `-1>>31 == -1`, `-1>>5 == -1`, `INT_MIN>>5 == -67108864`.\n\n**4. `/ 8` truncation — MATCH.** Both truncate toward zero: `-1/8==0`, `-7/8==0`, `-8/8==-1`, `-9/8==-1`, `INT_MIN/8==-268435456`. Covered by the exhaustive `step` sweep, which includes every negative numerator the expression can produce.\n\n**5. `-diff` vs `wrapping_neg()` — MATCH.** Exhaustive over all 2^32, including `INT_MIN → INT_MIN`.\n\n**6. Condition set and order — MATCH.** 1:1 statement alignment (C 6→Rust 44, 7→45, 8→48, 10→51, 12→56, 13→57, 14-19→58-63, 20→64, 21-23→65-67, 24-27→68-71, 30-56→76-125, 57-60→128-133, 61→135). The `lsbit` 3-way branch tests exact `== 4` first, then `& 1`, then else, in that order. The `&= ~1`-before-`|= (v>>1)&(v>>2)&1` sequencing (so the OR sees the already-masked value) is preserved; verified exhaustively over all 2^32. Both clamp tests `((uni ^ uni1) & !7) != 0` / `((uni ^ uni2) & !7) != 0` verified exhaustively over all 2^32 `uni`.\n\n**7. Final selection (the overwrite quirk) — MATCH, and empirically distinguished.** Rust lines 128-133 are literally `if d1 < d0 { uni = uni1; }` / `if d2 < d0 { uni = uni2; }` — both against the original `d0`, second able to overwrite the first. No running best. I also built a deliberate running-best variant and searched 400M random inputs: **960,097 cases** hit `d1 < d0 && d2 < d0 && uni1 != uni2`, where running-best returns `uni1` but C returns `uni2`. Rust matched C on **all 960,097**. Example: `(uni,step,pred,tgt,tgt2,lsbit) = (-24990, -1177023338, 181871, -33, 253852, 0)` → `d0=204800540, d1=45396256, d2=98650787`; C and Rust both return `-24991`, a running-best translation would return `-24989`.\n\n**8. Unused `p3` — no effect.** Declared at lib.c line 5, zero uses anywhere. Correctly omitted from the Rust. All other C locals (`uni1 uni2 diff p0 p1 p2 d0 d1 d2 d3`) are present.\n\n**9. Panic paths — none found.** Every add/sub/mul/neg uses `wrapping_*`. The only non-wrapping arithmetic is `/ 8` (constant divisor, neither `0` nor `-1`, so `INT_MIN/8` cannot trap) and shifts by constants 1/2/5/31 (all `< 32`, no shift-overflow panic). Empirically: 27.2M fuzz cases + a 61,843,584-case grid of extreme values (`INT_MIN`, `INT_MIN+1/+7/+8`, `INT_MAX`, `INT_MAX-1/-7/-8`, `0x7FFFFFF8`, `0x80000007`, `±2^28/2^29/2^30`, `lsbit ∈ {0..8, -1, INT_MIN, INT_MAX}`) run through a `-C overflow-checks=on -C debug-assertions=on` cdylib — zero aborts, zero mismatches.\n\n**10. FFI signature — MATCH.** `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\" fn encode_quant(uni, step, pred, tgt, tgt2, lsbit: c_int) -> c_int`, same parameter order as the header. `size_of::<c_int>() == size_of::<int>() == 4`. `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` is the *required* form in edition 2024 (Cargo.toml sets `edition = \"2024\"`; plain `#[no_mangle]` is a hard error there — I confirmed). Exported symbol tables are identical: exactly one `GLOBAL FUNC encode_quant` in each `.so`, matching `c_syms.txt`/`r_syms.txt`. All ~489M end-to-end cases were run through the real `dlopen`/`dlsym` FFI boundary, so the ABI is validated, not just inspected.\n\n**Total evidence: ~85.9 billion exhaustive sub-expression comparisons + ~489 million end-to-end calls across four builds (C `-O0`, C `-O2`, Rust `-O`, Rust `overflow-checks=on`). Zero differences.**\n\n### Unsure / caveats\n\n1. **C signed-overflow is UB, so \"the C's behavior\" is compiler-dependent.** Lines 30/36/42 (`* step`), 33/39/45 (`pred + diff`), 32/38/44 (`-diff`), 34/40/46/48/51/54 (`tgt - p`), and 50/53/56 (`d += `) can all overflow. The reference build uses `C_FLAGS = -fPIC` with **no `-O` flag** (per `c_src/build/CMakeFiles/harvest-work-s8YUb3.dir/flags.make`), where gcc 11.5 emits plain two's-complement wrapping — exactly what the Rust `wrapping_*` calls reproduce. I confirmed `-O0` and `-O2` agree on 27M cases and used `-O2 -fwrapv` for the exhaustive sweeps. I cannot rule out a *different* compiler/flag set exploiting the UB. This is a property of the C, not a defect in the translation; `wrapping_*` is the faithful choice.\n2. **`>>` on negative ints is implementation-defined in C** (not UB). Confirmed arithmetic on gcc 11.5 / x86-64. A hypothetical logical-shift C implementation would differ from Rust, but none exists in practice.\n3. **`extern \"C\" fn` installs an abort-on-unwind shim in Rust.** If a panic were ever reachable, Rust would abort where C would not. I found no reachable panic (item 9), so this is moot — but it is the one class of divergence that inspection alone can't fully close.\n4. **The full 6×32-bit input space (2^192) is not exhaustible.** My coverage was exhaustive *per sub-expression* over each sub-expression's complete domain, plus ~489M boundary-biased end-to-end cases, plus 1:1 statement alignment. That is strong evidence but not a formal proof of end-to-end equivalence.\n5. **I compiled with `rustc` directly rather than `cargo`** (same `--edition 2024`, both `-O` and `overflow-checks=on` variants), deliberately, to avoid writing into the repo as instructed. Cargo's `[profile.release] panic = \"abort\"` and `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` should not alter arithmetic semantics, but I did not exercise the exact cargo-produced artifact.\n\nNo files were modified; all scratch work was done under `$TMPDIR`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1356,"cache_read_input_tokens":71426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1356,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0166fexdEz6yfUVzz3kjTvvL","session_id":"6a91fdee-45d7-4693-8d7e-88ac17e9e078","uuid":"215e21ab-1e17-4e0e-8eeb-66813c384e73","timestamp":"2026-08-26T21:45:11.992Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Independent C-vs-Rust semantic audit"}
